Root `//#lint` and `//#typecheck-scripts` also have zero hits and 20 misses each. Root `//#lockfile:check` bypasses all 20 times. Total: **1,680 misses, 60 bypasses, zero hits across 1,740 tasks**. The bypasses are intentional and remain unchanged.

The workflow restores neither `.turbo` nor the pnpm store. GitHub's cache API reports zero cache entries at audit time. A unique commit key without a restore prefix would still miss on every new tree. A literal fixed key would stop updating after its first immutable cache save. A future local-cache design needs a unique save key and a compatible default-branch restore prefix.

## Ranked changes

These are estimates, not measurements of the changed workflow on GitHub. Monthly figures use an explicit scenario of 600 runs: 30 repetitions of this 20-run sample. They are not a forecast of team activity. All dollar savings are $0 on the current free public hosted runner.

| Rank | Change | Expected wall saving | Sample / scenario-month minutes saved | Risk and decision |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Two concurrent Turbo tasks in the existing job | Plan for 3 minutes per run; validate in CI | 60 / 1,800, estimated | The constrained matrix below has similar wall time at two and four, with more timeout margin at two. Implemented; no check is removed. |
| 2 | Cancel an older run of the same PR | No speedup to an isolated run | 7.95 / 238.5, counterfactual | Two overlapping superseded runs in this sample. Implemented; main pushes never cancel one another. |
| 3 | Restore a branch-scoped local Turbo cache | Unmeasured; theoretical ceiling below the 14.5-minute gate | Not safely estimable before input/output and restore-cost audit | Strong potential because every eligible task misses. A new cache action needs Joel's approval. Never let main consume PR-writable remote artifacts. |
| 4 | Reuse GitHub's green verdict for an identical main tree | Roughly the full main job on a verified match | At most 137.38 / 4,121.5 in this sample | Nine main trees equal earlier green PR-head trees. This is a candidate count, not complete proof of the PR checkout tree. Joel must approve moving main's proof to the PR verdict. Not implemented. |
| 5 | Split check, test, and build into jobs | Unmeasured; may overlap work like rank 1 | Not estimated | Each job repeats checkout/install. Tests already depend on builds; infra tests depend on web tests. Preserve the required `checks` aggregate if pursued. Not implemented. |
| 6 | Shard site tests in two | Ideal ceiling about 1.66 minutes before repeated setup | At most 33.2 / 996, idealized | Assumes perfect division of 199.18 seconds; not a measured result. Requires proving complete test coverage and accounting for repeated setup. Not implemented. |
| 7 | Cache the pnpm store | Less than 13 seconds before restore/save overhead | Less than 4.33 / 130 | Too small to justify a new action. Existing setup-node caching needs pnpm available before its cache lookup; do not silently assume the runner's bootstrap corepack. Not implemented. |
| 8 | Larger runner | Unmeasured | Not estimated | Same work, unknown speedup, changed cost. Requires Joel's sign-off. Not implemented. |
| Rejected | Skip `.brain` or news-only changes | No safe saving established | 0 credited | `.brain/**` feeds content generation, typecheck, build, and tests. Code excerpts and snapshots also read repository content. No skip path is proven safe. |
| Rejected | Shared PR-writable Turbo remote cache | No saving credited | 0 credited | A PR could plant an artifact later consumed on main. Signing does not remove same-repository PR write authority. |

The concurrency estimate starts with the actual task graph from `pnpm turbo run check test build --dry=json`. Grouped serial CI logs are buffered, so gaps between successive completed groups provide approximate task costs, not exact timings. Scheduling those costs on four ideal workers gives a 362.3-second median versus 871.4 seconds on one worker. This ignores resource contention and is an optimistic bound. The plan credits only three minutes of that potential 8.5-minute saving, pending hosted CI. The constrained replay below determines the chosen concurrency.

The superseded-run estimate credits only time after the next run of the same PR begins. Runs `41904984907` and `41776246059` overlap their replacements. Cancellation latency and work before the replacement arrives reduce actual savings. Savings from different rows overlap and must not be added together.

## Initial local experiment, insufficient for runner selection

The serial baseline and four-way replay each use a distinct, initially empty Turbo cache directory. Both run the complete graph with `CI=true`, `--summarize`, and `/usr/bin/time -p`. Dependency installation and pinned-object fetching happen before the timed runs. Existing installed dependencies and source objects remain warm; this is a cold **Turbo-cache** replay, not a clean-machine install benchmark.

| Configuration | Successful tasks | Cached tasks | Exit | Elapsed seconds |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `--concurrency=1` | 87 / 87 | 0 / 87 | 0 | 452.94 |
| `--concurrency=4` | 87 / 87 | 0 / 87 | 0 | 182.88 |

Four-way execution saves **270.06 seconds locally**, a **59.6% reduction** in elapsed time. Aggregate user CPU time rises from 797.04 to 820.07 seconds, consistent with overlapping work rather than removing it. There is one observation per configuration, not a reliability study.

Both runs use pnpm 11.3.0 and local Node 24.22.1; CI pins Node 24.18.0. The local worker environment caps Vitest at four workers, while site and infra retain their explicit single-worker settings. The host differs from the GitHub runner. The serial run starts before this audit note exists; the parallel run includes it. Product sources and task definitions are unchanged. Do not extrapolate this local ratio directly to CI.

The step measurements rule out dependency installation as the main bottleneck. The replay supports overlapping the graph. It does not rule out hosted-runner memory pressure or sandbox contention. The first real PR run remains the deciding experiment.

## Four-core, 16-GB matrix

**Choose two concurrent tasks.** The median falls from 479.33 seconds at one to 264.56 seconds at two. Four takes 270.61 seconds. The worst test uses 39.13% of its timeout at two, versus 56.34% at four. Similar wall time with more margin favors two.

All nine runs use benchmark commit **`6882060a6d1d036923eebd9c7bab26f4f5a9bd03`**. Every `6882060` in the tables refers to that exact snapshot-only checkpoint. It remains an ancestor of the final change. The first attempt stopped at a stale Git snapshot before completing the gate; none of those partial rows count. Refreshing and committing the snapshot fixed the measurement setup.

Each run executes `pnpm turbo run check test build --force --concurrency=N` under `taskset -c 0-3` and an unprivileged `systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=16G`. Frozen installation and `pnpm sources:fetch` pass before the batch. Runs interleave one, two, and four three times. The coordinator checks the commit and clean worktree before every run.

A read-only Vitest reporter records every case's duration and resolved timeout. A Node preload adds that reporter only to Vitest CLI processes. It changes no test, assertion, timeout, installed package, or worker configuration. The row's inherited worker override is removed, so Vitest uses its four-CPU default of three workers; site and infra retain one. Every report confirms CPU affinity `0-3` and the 17,179,869,184-byte memory limit.

All nine gates pass **87/87 tasks with zero Turbo cache hits**. Each records **1,496 passed tests**, for **13,464 test executions**. There are no retries, flaky cases, unknown budgets, or tests above 60% of their timeout. All three concurrency settings meet the original headroom rule. Two is the final choice because four shows no clear wall-time advantage and uses more of its worst test's budget. No web worker cap is added.

Times are seconds. Load shows the one-, five-, and fifteen-minute averages recorded by `uptime` immediately before each run. Observed memory peaks are GiB from the scoped reporters.

| Run | Tree | Wall | Gate | Tests | Worst budget use | Pre-run load | Observed peak GiB |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| c1r1 | 6882060 | 426.03 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 22.64% | 7.47 / 7.79 / 8.58 | 4.10 |
| c2r1 | 6882060 | 256.34 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 25.65% | 10.30 / 11.41 / 10.04 | 7.01 |
| c4r1 | 6882060 | 239.04 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 35.36% | 9.38 / 10.37 / 9.94 | 8.30 |
| c1r2 | 6882060 | 479.33 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 22.95% | 15.84 / 14.34 / 11.75 | 4.42 |
| c2r2 | 6882060 | 264.56 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 31.31% | 8.78 / 9.63 / 10.54 | 6.14 |
| c4r2 | 6882060 | 270.61 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 38.18% | 31.53 / 18.47 / 13.65 | 8.99 |
| c1r3 | 6882060 | 621.66 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 32.23% | 41.67 / 31.94 / 20.68 | 4.26 |
| c2r3 | 6882060 | 405.14 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 39.13% | 50.27 / 50.35 / 33.85 | 5.91 |
| c4r3 | 6882060 | 381.94 | pass, 87/87 | 1,496 | 56.34% | 69.38 / 67.95 / 48.79 | 8.57 |

Four runs start above load 16; these noisy observations are retained and marked here rather than discarded. Shared CPU load rises through the batch, so the median comparison does not establish a causal speed difference between two and four. Relative to one, two saves 214.77 seconds at the observed medians, or 44.8%. This is a local measurement, not a hosted-CI forecast.

### Five slowest tests in every run

The case keys below retain each full test name and its file. A cell shows `key: duration / timeout (budget used)`, in seconds. These are the five slowest cases by elapsed duration, not by percentage of their timeout.

- **PB**: `apps/web/test/production-bundle.test.ts`, “production bundle > statically renders home and excludes server devtools from browser and Worker output”.
- **DB**: `apps/web/test/production-bundle.test.ts`, “production bundle > would contain them in a development build, so the checks can fail”.
- **RPC**: `apps/infra/test/website-build.test.ts`, “Alchemy selects the reader and RpcBackend evaluates without import.meta.url to serve native RPC”.
- **RULE**: `packages/core/test/patterns-rule.test.ts`, “rat-stack pattern rules > keeps sleeping loops in machines without flagging unrelated sleep functions”.
- **HEADERS**: `apps/site/test/reader-asset-headers.test.ts`, “preview output cannot leave indexing headers or preview canonicals in a later production build”.
- **FLAGS**: `apps/site/test/flag-ledger-runtime.test.ts`, “committed positions follow an independent ladder model, and failed commits never manufacture receipts”.
- **MODEL**: `packages/deploy/test/machine.model.test.ts`, “deployment command model > generated step histories stop on refusal, partial apply, failure or crash; terminal probes cannot restart work”.
- **DEV**: `apps/web/test/dev-server.test.ts`, “vite dev > serves the search page”.

