The callable-tools case has no `-32603` failures. The dev-server case remains below 22% of its 30-second budget in every run. These observations do not erase the historical contention failures; they measure today's source and current worker settings.

### Limits of the matrix

This is the approved CPU-affinity alternative to Docker, not a hardware-identical hosted runner. Local Node is 24.22.1; CI uses 24.18.0. The physical CPU differs, other workers share the pinned cores, and Vite's generated disk caches remain warm. Turbo work is forced, but that does not remove Vite caches. Trash is unsupported on the source mount, so no destructive cache-clearing workaround is used. The high-load rows are pessimistic observations, while warm caches and different hardware can be optimistic. Hosted PR CI remains the final proof.

## Fence and rollback

The active main ruleset requires the exact `checks` name and an up-to-date branch. This change preserves that job name, both event triggers, all 87 tasks, dependency ordering, strict checking, tests, and builds. It changes no dependency, runner label, permission, timeout, diagnostic, or hook. No path or main-tree skip is introduced.

The workflow's concurrency group uses the PR number when present and the unique run id otherwise. Only pull-request events cancel an earlier run. A later push must still complete the full required check before merging. Main pushes remain independent.

The workflow selects `--concurrency=2`, with the benchmark SHA and all three medians in its comment. Rollback consists of returning the gate to `--concurrency=1`; the PR cancellation can remain or be reverted separately. No deployment or flag change is involved.

## Follow-up proof

The desk opens the PR; this worker does not push. The first PR run must complete all 87 tasks successfully on `ubuntu-latest`. Compare its gate step with the PR-specific 768-second median and 939-second p90; the all-event median is 871.5 seconds. Inspect sandbox failures, pool contention, and memory pressure. A green local run does not establish performance on the hosted runner.

Measure again after merge, keeping PR and main runs separate. Do not describe the estimate as a shipped speedup before those measurements exist.

